 17592186161929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050133 #edb/ref 1031 "Spearman's Rank Correlation Coefficient" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050133 #edb/ref 1033 3020 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050133 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "2f80777c-c26a-5832-afd6-879984e6f26d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050406 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048424 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050406 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050406 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050406 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050406 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050406 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186204008 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050406 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186204009 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050406 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186204010 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050406 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186204011 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050406 #edb/ref 1031 "Sums of Squares" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050406 #edb/ref 1033 5204 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050406 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "467c6e31-0c6e-5017-92e2-f1c8d38e5a4c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093536 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186093537 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093536 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186093538 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093536 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186093539 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093536 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186093540 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093536 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186093541 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093536 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093536 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093536 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d47e0d00-6213-58d4-b4c4-0da7914c3883" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093537 #edb/ref 1080 "ρ =-0.9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093537 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093537 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "98692c5f-37d3-5a0f-930e-cb9d9b4520b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093538 #edb/ref 1080 "ρ = 1.8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093538 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093538 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "afdb47a2-d0ed-5d21-9d9a-e1a39e6ef77f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093539 #edb/ref 1080 "ρ = 0.9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093539 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093539 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d9bc8f31-029d-5a00-aa57-f5873a3f9d9a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093540 #edb/ref 1080 "ρ =-0.3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093540 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093540 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2d4c2c3d-ab04-5d79-9f09-78335d2dd61b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093541 #edb/ref 1080 "ρ =-0.6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093541 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093541 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3309670c-8f47-5743-834c-bf27120f0f5a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096518 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5711" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096518 #edb/ref 1071 "A stall selling lemonade is set up at a special four-day summer festival. The stall owner wants to check the strength of the linear correlation between $x$, the temperature at $12\\,\\textrm{p.m}$, and $y$, the number of cups of lemonade sold that day. The data is shown below.\n\n| **Temperature at<br>$12$ p.m. ($^\\circ \\mathrm{C}$)** | **$26$** | **$30$** | **$22$** | **$18$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **Cups of Lemonade Sold** | $100$ | $118$ | $124$ | $86$ |\n\nYou are given that the means are $\\overline{x}=24$ and $\\overline{y}=107$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\rho\\approx 0.54$\n2. There is a strong positive correlation between $x$ and $y$.\n3. There is a weak positive correlation between $x$ and $y$.\n\n*Hint: $\\mid \\rho \\mid > 0.7$ indicates a strong correlation, and $0.3 \\leq \\mid \\rho \\mid \\leq 0.7$ indicates a weak correlation.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096518 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "aa7ce13a-2f5f-55f5-bac0-ced1a321d5b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096518 #edb/ref 1074 "The linear correlation coefficient $\\rho$ of the data sets\n\n$$\nx=\\big\\{x_1,x_2, \\ldots, x_n \\big\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=\\big\\{y_1,y_2, \\ldots, y_n \\big\\}\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\rho(x,y) = \\dfrac{\\textrm{Cov}(x,y)}{\\sigma_x \\cdot \\sigma_y} = \\dfrac{S_{xy}}{\\sqrt{S_{xx} \\cdot S_{yy}}}\n$$\n\nIn addition, we have the following rule of thumb:\n\n- if $\\mid \\rho \\mid \\gt 0.7$, the correlation is *strong*\n- if $0.3\\leq \\mid \\rho \\mid \\leq 0.7$, the correlation is *weak*\n- if $\\mid \\rho \\mid \\lt 0.3$, then the correlation is *negligible*\n\nLet's now examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. To calculate $\\rho$, we first compute the sum in the numerator:\n$S_{xy}|= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))(y_{i} - y^(―)); = (26 - 24)(100 - 107) + (30 - 24)(118 - 107); = + (22 - 24)(124 - 107) + (18 - 24)(86 - 107); = (2)(-7) + (6)(11) + (-2)(17) + (-6)(-21); =-14 + 66 - 34 + 126; = 144$\nSecond, we compute the sums in the denominator:\n$S_{xx}|= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))^{2}; = (26 - 24)^{2} + (30 - 24)^{2} + (22 - 24)^{2} + (18 - 24)^{2}; = 4 + 36 + 4 + 36; = 80; S_{yy}|= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(y_{i} - y^(―))^{2}; = (100 - 107)^{2} + (118 - 107)^{2} + (124 - 107)^{2} + (86 - 107)^{2}; = 49 + 121 + 289 + 441; = 900$\nFinally, we use the formula:\n$ρ(x, y)|= \\frac{144}{\\sqrt{80} \\cdot \\sqrt{900}}; \\approx \\frac{144}{8.944272 \\cdot 30}; \\approx 0.54$,\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Since $0.3\\leq \\mid \\rho \\mid \\leq 0.7$ the correlation is weak. Also, since $\\rho$ is positive, the correlation is positive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096522 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5725" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096522 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following data sets:\n$x = \\big\\{6, \\: 3, \\: -1, \\: 0 \\big\\},\\qquad y = \\big\\{5, \\: 7, \\: -2, \\: 6 \\big\\}$\nYou're given that $\\overline{x}=2$, $\\overline{y}=4$, $\\sigma_x \\approx 2.739$, and $\\sigma_y \\approx 3.536$. Calculate the corresponding linear correlation coefficient. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096522 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5002af3e-d0f0-59eb-a52d-bfd7d101115e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096522 #edb/ref 1074 "The (population) linear correlation coefficient of the data sets\n\n$$\nx=\\big\\{x_1,x_2, \\ldots, x_n \\big\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=\\big\\{y_1,y_2, \\ldots, y_n \\big\\}\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\rho(x,y) = \\dfrac{\\textrm{Cov}(x,y)}{\\sigma_x \\cdot \\sigma_y}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\textrm{Cov}(x,y)$ is the covariance, and $\\sigma_x$ and $\\sigma_y$ are the standard deviations of the data sets.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the covariance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nCov(x, y) &= \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))(y_{i} - y^(―)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}[(6 - 2)(5 - 4) + (3 - 2)(7 - 4) + (-1 - 2)(-2 - 4) + (0 - 2)(6 - 4)] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}[(4)(1) + (1)(3) + (-3)(-6) + (-2)(2)] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}[4 + 3 + 18 - 4] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot 21 \\\\\n&= 5.25\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the formula. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nρ(x, y) &= (Cov(x, y))/(σ_{x} \\cdot σ_{y}) \\\\\n&\\approx \\frac{5.25}{2.739 \\cdot 3.536} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.54\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096593 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9710" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096593 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following data sets:\n$x = \\big\\{13, \\: 10, \\: 9, \\: 12 \\big\\},\\qquad y = \\big\\{-1, \\: 3, \\: 2, \\: 4 \\big\\}$\nYou are given that $\\overline{x}=11$ and $\\overline{y}=2$. Calculate the corresponding linear correlation coefficient. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096593 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8c2381d6-a9af-5ce3-aa20-d0e19a7a7229" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096593 #edb/ref 1074 "The linear correlation coefficient $\\rho$ of the data sets\n\n$$\nx=\\big\\{x_1,x_2, \\ldots, x_n \\big\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=\\big\\{y_1,y_2, \\ldots, y_n \\big\\}\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\rho(x,y) = \\dfrac{\\textrm{Cov}(x,y)}{\\sigma_x \\cdot \\sigma_y} = \\dfrac{S_{xy}}{\\sqrt{S_{xx} \\cdot S_{yy}}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the sum in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{xy} &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))(y_{i} - y^(―)) \\\\\n&= (13 - 11)(-1 - 2) + (10 - 11)(3 - 2) \\\\\n&= + (9 - 11)(2 - 2) + (12 - 11)(4 - 2) \\\\\n&= (2)(-3) + (-1)(1) + (-2)(0) + (1)(2) \\\\\n&=-6 - 1 + 0 + 2 \\\\\n&=-5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSecond, we compute the sums in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{xx} &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))^{2} \\\\\n&= (13 - 11)^{2} + (10 - 11)^{2} + (9 - 11)^{2} + (12 - 11)^{2} \\\\\n&= 4 + 1 + 4 + 1 \\\\\n&= 10 \\\\\nS_{yy} &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(y_{i} - y^(―))^{2} \\\\\n&= (-1 - 2)^{2} + (3 - 2)^{2} + (2 - 2)^{2} + (4 - 2)^{2} \\\\\n&= 9 + 1 + 0 + 4 \\\\\n&= 14\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nρ(x, y) &= ((-5))/(\\sqrt{10} \\cdot \\sqrt{14}) \\\\\n&\\approx - \\frac{5}{3.162278 \\cdot 3.741657} \\\\\n&\\approx - 0.42\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096726 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186096940 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096726 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186097075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096726 #edb/ref 1039 "Interpreting Linear Correlation Coefficients in Context" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096726 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186096518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096726 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e2f40eb3-6087-5c38-b7c6-e160ff925bc6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096727 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048914 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096727 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096727 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096727 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186096927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096727 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186097073 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096727 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing the Correlation Coefficient Using Covariance and Standard Deviations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096727 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186096522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096727 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "76569338-1e2a-5b12-96ef-685d272343cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096786 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050406 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096786 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186097074 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096786 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186097077 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096786 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing the Linear Correlation Coefficient Using the Expanded Formula" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096786 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186096593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096786 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "dd2caf12-80c2-553a-9737-64151565cf86" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096858 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096858 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096858 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096858 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096858 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097695 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096858 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097717 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096858 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097718 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096858 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096858 #edb/ref 1045 "The Linear Correlation Coefficient" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096858 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186097603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096858 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048424 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096858 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "56d16c42-7bff-5947-a997-ebd77e5b8a2e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096927 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186093536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096927 #edb/ref 1069 "q-10271" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096927 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the data sets $x$ and $y$ such that $Cov(x, y) =-6$ and their standard deviations are $σ_{x} = 2$ and $σ_{y} = 5$. Compute the corresponding linear correlation coefficient.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096927 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2811c370-5b30-5984-8372-c002bcffbf44" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096927 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097603 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097717 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097603 #edb/ref 1061 20623 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097603 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8f8585ea-c69b-57cc-9474-2e65fa5ad893" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097603 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186097862 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097604 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097718 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097604 #edb/ref 1061 20625 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097604 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "389e6644-1f27-50f3-821c-27b03f4bc7d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097604 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097606 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097695 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097606 #edb/ref 1061 20709 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097606 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0575b9a0-d372-5de1-9411-b558a65a2b50" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097606 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097694 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097694 #edb/ref 1061 36411 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097694 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d5b72b77-a57c-507c-b755-bdf8208dc2e8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097694 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096786 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097695 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097695 #edb/ref 1061 36415 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097695 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2e0466e2-c1f8-573f-bdcb-205404d8cec2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097695 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186097891 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097717 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097717 #edb/ref 1061 39739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097717 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c58771bb-e2f1-5f3c-9354-320e7568cc2d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097717 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186097902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097718 #edb/ref 1061 39740 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097718 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "be8ab20d-d46f-516f-aba3-1048baeb14b1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097718 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186097903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097862 #edb/ref 1064 "The **linear correlation coefficient,** denoted $\\rho$ (the Greek letter \"rho\"), is a number that gives the strength of the *linear* dependence between two sets of paired observations $(x,y)$.\n\nThe linear correlation coefficient satisfies the following constraint:\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq \\rho \\leq 1\n$$\n\nWe interpret the endpoints of this interval as follows:\n\n- $\\rho = 1$ means that there is a perfect *positive* correlation between the data points.\n- $\\rho = -1$ means that there is a perfect *negative* correlation between the data points.\n\nMoreover, $\\rho = 0$ means there is no *linear* correlation between the data points.\n\nIn addition, we have the following rule of thumb:\n\n- If $\\mid \\rho \\mid \\gt 0.7$, the correlation is **strong,** and the data points closely follow a straight line.![](images/3f/3fd8b4e85bd76bbf78fdb2b135c49b3fd5e37fd9651dcae29d945d75aa45b905.png)\n\n- If $0.3\\leq \\mid \\rho \\mid \\leq 0.7$, the correlation is **weak.** The data points follow a straight line, but many are far from the line.![](images/c1/c132ec90770bb84ad6c7dcc4b24773a923139c616f523ba0a23c1506c40c508c.png)\n\n- If $\\mid \\rho \\mid \\lt 0.3$, then the correlation is **negligible.** There is no (linear) correlation.![](images/9a/9afa80bfadc40b8b832b17dcea10d66d5a08955909d5bea66c7c010558b5ea19.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097862 #edb/ref 1065 3699 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097862 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "372f5b4c-9826-52f6-a70c-5e3a035de64e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097862 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097891 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we have data sets\n\n$$\nx=\\big\\{x_1,x_2, \\ldots, x_n \\big\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=\\big\\{y_1,y_2, \\ldots, y_n \\big\\}\n$$\n\nof the same size.\n\nLet's now introduce the following notation:\n\n$$\nS_{xx} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})^2, \\qquad S_{yy} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i-\\overline{y})^2, \\qquad S_{xy} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})(y_i-\\overline{y})\n$$\n\nNotice that $S_{xx}, S_{yy}$, and $S_{xy}$ are the sums of square deviations found in the formulas for the variance of $x$, the variance of $y$, and covariance of $x$ and $y$, respectively.\n\nUsing the previous definition, we can show that the correlation coefficient can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\rho = \\dfrac{S_{xy}}{\\sqrt{S_{xx} \\cdot S_{yy}}}\\,\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097891 #edb/ref 1065 5966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097891 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1ab8695a-49cb-5bc1-8934-ffd2ff28261e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097891 #edb/ref 1067 "The Linear Correlation Coefficient in Sigma Notation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097902 #edb/ref 1064 "To calculate $\\rho$, we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nρ(x, y) &= (Cov(x, y))/(σ_{x} \\cdot σ_{y})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\textrm{Cov}(x,y)$ is the covariance between $x$ and $y$, and\n- $\\sigma_x$ and $\\sigma_y$ are the standard deviations of $x$ and $y$, respectively.\n\nFor example, consider data sets $x$ and $y$ such that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}(x,y)=-9, \\qquad \\sigma_x=6, \\qquad \\sigma_y=2\n$$\n\nSubstituting our numbers into the formula above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nρ(x, y) &= ((-9))/((6) \\cdot (2)) \\\\\n&= ((-9))/(12) \\\\\n&=-0.75\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mid \\rho \\mid \\gt 0.7$, the correlation is strong. Also, since $\\rho$ is negative, the correlation is negative.\n\nTherefore, there is a strong negative correlation between $x$ and $y$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097902 #edb/ref 1065 6586 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097902 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6f296964-c1ff-5379-861d-1a9809ca7cc6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097902 #edb/ref 1067 "Calculating the Correlation Coefficient" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097903 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's derive the following result:\n\n$$\n\\rho = \\dfrac{S_{xy}}{\\sqrt{S_{xx} \\cdot S_{yy}}}\n$$\n\nWe start with the definition of the correlation coefficient $\\rho$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nρ &= (Cov(x, y))/(σ_{x} \\cdot σ_{y})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}(x,y) = \\dfrac1n \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})(y_i-\\overline{y}) = \\dfrac{S_{xy}}{n}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nσ_{x} &= \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))^{2}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{S_{xx}}{n}} \\\\\nσ_{y} &= \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)(y_{i} - y^(―))^{2}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{S_{yy}}{n}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our definition for $\\rho$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nρ &= (Cov(x, y))/(σ_{x} \\cdot σ_{y}) \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{S_{xy}}{n}))/(\\sqrt{\\frac{S_{xx}}{n}} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{S_{yy}}{n}}) \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{S_{xy}}{n}))/(\\sqrt{\\frac{S_{xx} \\cdot S_{yy}}{n^{2}}}) \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{S_{xy}}{n}))/((\\frac{\\sqrt{S_{xx} \\cdot S_{yy}}}{n})) \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{S_{xy}}{n}))/((\\frac{\\sqrt{S_{xx} \\cdot S_{yy}}}{n})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{S_{xy}}{\\sqrt{S_{xx} \\cdot S_{yy}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097903 #edb/ref 1065 6587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097903 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "219dd7c3-97f9-5504-ad97-d8710a6cf5bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097903 #edb/ref 1067 "Proving the Formula for Rho in Terms of Squared Deviations" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
